1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,655
An onion grows with every new layer.

2
00:00:02,655 --> 00:00:04,949
A balloon shrinks when you squeeze it.

3
00:00:04,949 --> 00:00:07,153
A magnet pulls paper clips in tight.

4
00:00:07,553 --> 00:00:09,163
Layers make things bigger.

5
00:00:09,163 --> 00:00:11,285
A stronger pull makes them smaller.

6
00:00:11,735 --> 00:00:14,302
Diya is peeling an onion in her kitchen.

7
00:00:14,302 --> 00:00:16,229
More layers, a bigger onion.

8
00:00:16,579 --> 00:00:18,147
Atoms have layers too.

9
00:00:18,147 --> 00:00:19,154
Their shells.

10
00:00:19,154 --> 00:00:22,192
But some atoms with more
electrons are smaller.

11
00:00:22,692 --> 00:00:26,538
By the end of this video, you will
know why a fluorine atom is smaller

12
00:00:26,538 --> 00:00:27,797
than a lithium atom.

13
00:00:28,447 --> 00:00:30,099
So how big is an atom?

14
00:00:30,449 --> 00:00:32,599
We cannot measure one atom alone.

15
00:00:32,599 --> 00:00:35,823
So we measure the distance
between two joined atoms.

16
00:00:36,173 --> 00:00:40,661
Half the distance between the nuclei
of two bonded atoms is the covalent

17
00:00:40,661 --> 00:00:41,561
radius.

18
00:00:41,330 --> 00:00:44,207
A nucleus is the tiny centre of the atom.

19
00:00:44,557 --> 00:00:49,198
In a metal, half the distance between
two neighbouring atoms is the metallic

20
00:00:49,198 --> 00:00:50,098
radius.

21
00:00:49,845 --> 00:00:54,025
Radii are measured in picometres,
a million millionth of a metre.

22
00:00:54,625 --> 00:00:57,577
Now watch period two
on our molecule bench.

23
00:00:57,577 --> 00:00:59,791
We go from lithium to fluorine.

24
00:01:00,141 --> 00:01:03,079
Lithium has three protons in its nucleus.

25
00:01:03,079 --> 00:01:05,801
Its outer electron sits in shell two.

26
00:01:05,801 --> 00:01:08,782
Radius, one hundred fifty two picometres.

27
00:01:09,182 --> 00:01:11,058
Beryllium has four protons.

28
00:01:11,058 --> 00:01:13,415
Same shell, but a stronger pull.

29
00:01:13,415 --> 00:01:14,822
One hundred eleven.

30
00:01:15,222 --> 00:01:16,796
Boron, eighty eight.

31
00:01:16,796 --> 00:01:18,473
Carbon, seventy seven.

32
00:01:18,473 --> 00:01:20,201
Nitrogen, seventy four.

33
00:01:20,601 --> 00:01:22,180
Oxygen, sixty six.

34
00:01:22,180 --> 00:01:23,924
Fluorine, sixty four.

35
00:01:24,324 --> 00:01:27,100
Each step adds one
proton, but no new shell.

36
00:01:27,100 --> 00:01:29,642
So the pull grows, and the atom shrinks.

37
00:01:30,292 --> 00:01:31,971
Now go down group one.

38
00:01:31,971 --> 00:01:35,280
Lithium, sodium, potassium,
rubidium, caesium.

39
00:01:35,680 --> 00:01:37,959
Each step adds a whole new shell.

40
00:01:37,959 --> 00:01:39,423
So the radius grows.

41
00:01:39,423 --> 00:01:43,559
From one hundred fifty two, to
two hundred sixty two picometres.

42
00:01:44,209 --> 00:01:45,846
Now let's make ions.

43
00:01:45,846 --> 00:01:49,839
An ion is an atom that has
lost or gained electrons.

44
00:01:50,239 --> 00:01:52,768
Sodium loses its one outer electron.

45
00:01:52,768 --> 00:01:54,982
Its whole third shell is gone.

46
00:01:54,982 --> 00:01:59,244
The radius drops from one eighty
six to ninety five picometres.

47
00:01:59,644 --> 00:02:01,696
Fluorine gains one electron.

48
00:02:01,696 --> 00:02:05,138
Same nine protons, more
electrons pushing apart.

49
00:02:05,138 --> 00:02:08,829
The radius grows from sixty
four to one thirty six.

50
00:02:09,479 --> 00:02:10,898
Now look closely.

51
00:02:10,898 --> 00:02:15,231
The fluoride ion and the sodium
ion both have ten electrons.

52
00:02:15,631 --> 00:02:16,842
Pause and think.

53
00:02:16,842 --> 00:02:20,639
Which one is smaller, the
fluoride ion or the sodium ion?

54
00:02:23,839 --> 00:02:25,159
The sodium ion!

55
00:02:25,159 --> 00:02:29,005
It has eleven protons pulling
the same ten electrons.

56
00:02:29,005 --> 00:02:30,838
Fluoride has only nine.

57
00:02:31,488 --> 00:02:35,663
Species with the same number of
electrons are called isoelectronic.

58
00:02:35,663 --> 00:02:37,810
Let's rank them, step by step.

59
00:02:38,160 --> 00:02:39,116
Step one.

60
00:02:39,116 --> 00:02:40,783
Count the electrons.

61
00:02:40,783 --> 00:02:45,447
Oxide, fluoride, sodium ion
and magnesium ion all have ten.

62
00:02:46,197 --> 00:02:47,097
Step two.

63
00:02:47,011 --> 00:02:48,332
Count the protons.

64
00:02:48,332 --> 00:02:50,615
Eight, nine, eleven and twelve.

65
00:02:51,365 --> 00:02:52,318
Step three.

66
00:02:52,318 --> 00:02:55,291
More protons pull the
same electrons closer.

67
00:02:55,291 --> 00:02:57,258
So the radius gets smaller.

68
00:02:58,008 --> 00:02:58,944
Step four.

69
00:02:58,944 --> 00:03:04,577
So, oxide is the biggest, then fluoride,
then the sodium ion, and the magnesium

70
00:03:04,577 --> 00:03:06,179
ion is the smallest.

71
00:03:06,929 --> 00:03:08,134
Remember this.

72
00:03:08,134 --> 00:03:10,433
Same electrons, more protons.

73
00:03:10,433 --> 00:03:11,707
A smaller ion.

74
00:03:12,407 --> 00:03:14,142
Now, one worked example.

75
00:03:14,442 --> 00:03:19,445
In a chlorine molecule, the two nuclei
are one hundred ninety eight picometres

76
00:03:19,445 --> 00:03:20,345
apart.

77
00:03:20,077 --> 00:03:22,625
Find the covalent radius of chlorine.

78
00:03:23,125 --> 00:03:26,737
The covalent radius is half the
distance between the nuclei.

79
00:03:27,487 --> 00:03:31,996
One hundred ninety eight divided
by two is ninety nine picometres.

80
00:03:32,746 --> 00:03:36,957
In copper metal, neighbouring nuclei
are two hundred fifty six picometres

81
00:03:36,957 --> 00:03:37,857
apart.

82
00:03:37,533 --> 00:03:41,555
Half of that, one hundred twenty
eight, is the metallic radius.

83
00:03:42,305 --> 00:03:46,224
Chlorine at ninety nine is bigger
than fluorine at sixty four.

84
00:03:46,224 --> 00:03:49,137
It is one shell further
down group seventeen.

85
00:03:49,787 --> 00:03:51,252
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:03:51,602 --> 00:03:55,904
Some students think ions with the
same number of electrons must be the

87
00:03:55,904 --> 00:03:56,804
same size.

88
00:03:57,168 --> 00:03:59,217
But the protons are different.

89
00:03:59,217 --> 00:04:01,105
More protons, smaller ion.

90
00:04:01,105 --> 00:04:05,522
Oxide is bigger than fluoride,
which is bigger than the sodium ion.

91
00:04:06,172 --> 00:04:07,914
Where is this asked?

92
00:04:07,914 --> 00:04:13,469
NEET and JEE Main both ask you to order
the sizes of atoms and isoelectronic

93
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:14,369
ions.

94
00:04:14,723 --> 00:04:16,050
Exam level now.

95
00:04:16,050 --> 00:04:19,840
Sulphide, chloride, potassium
ion and calcium ion.

96
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:22,381
Which order of radius is correct?

97
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:23,764
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:29,164 --> 00:04:30,557
The answer is B.

99
00:04:30,557 --> 00:04:35,084
Sulphide, then chloride, then
potassium ion, then calcium ion.

100
00:04:35,584 --> 00:04:37,976
All four have eighteen electrons.

101
00:04:37,976 --> 00:04:39,694
They are isoelectronic.

102
00:04:40,194 --> 00:04:44,040
Their protons are sixteen,
seventeen, nineteen and twenty.

103
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:46,568
More protons, smaller ion.

104
00:04:46,568 --> 00:04:51,149
So sulphide is the largest, and
the calcium ion is the smallest.

105
00:04:51,149 --> 00:04:52,909
A is exactly backwards.

106
00:04:53,509 --> 00:04:54,970
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:54,970 --> 00:04:56,471
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:59,671 --> 00:05:02,669
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:05:03,069 --> 00:05:03,969
One.

110
00:05:03,571 --> 00:05:06,502
Why do atoms shrink from
lithium to fluorine?

111
00:05:07,302 --> 00:05:08,202
Two.

112
00:05:07,852 --> 00:05:12,076
Why is the sodium ion so much
smaller than the sodium atom?

113
00:05:12,876 --> 00:05:13,776
Three.

114
00:05:13,516 --> 00:05:17,942
Which is bigger, the oxide ion
or the magnesium ion, and why?

115
00:05:18,842 --> 00:05:23,511
Next time: why does it take more energy
to pull an electron from fluorine

116
00:05:23,511 --> 00:05:24,850
than from lithium?

117
00:05:24,850 --> 00:05:27,225
We find out in ionization enthalpy.
